### Knowing What Your App Is Doing in Production

You can't fix what you can't see. Observability is the ability to understand your system's internal state from external outputs. It has three pillars: **Logs**, **Metrics**, and **Traces**.

#### Structured Logging

Plain text logs are hard to search. Structured logs (JSON) can be queried, filtered, and aggregated by tools like Datadog, Loki, or CloudWatch.

```js
import pino from 'pino';

const logger = pino({
  level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info',
  // In production, output raw JSON
  // In dev, use pino-pretty for human-readable output
  transport: process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production'
    ? { target: 'pino-pretty' }
    : undefined,
});

export default logger;
```

```js
// Log with context, not just strings
logger.info({ userId: req.user.id, action: 'login' }, 'User logged in');
logger.error({ err, orderId }, 'Payment processing failed');

// Output:
// { level: 30, time: 1711234567890, userId: 42, action: "login", msg: "User logged in" }
```

#### Request Correlation IDs

Attach a unique ID to every request so you can trace it through all logs:

```js
import { randomUUID } from 'crypto';

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  // Use existing correlation ID from upstream, or generate new one
  req.correlationId = req.headers['x-correlation-id'] || randomUUID();
  res.setHeader('x-correlation-id', req.correlationId);

  // Create a child logger with the correlation ID bound
  req.log = logger.child({ correlationId: req.correlationId });
  next();
});

// Now every log from this request is traceable
app.get('/api/orders/:id', async (req, res) => {
  req.log.info({ orderId: req.params.id }, 'Fetching order');
  // ...
});
```

#### Health Check Endpoint

Every production service must expose a health check endpoint for load balancers and orchestrators:

```js
app.get('/health', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // Check all critical dependencies
    await db.raw('SELECT 1');          // DB is reachable
    await redis.ping();                // Cache is reachable

    res.json({
      status: 'healthy',
      uptime: process.uptime(),
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    });
  } catch (err) {
    // Return 503 if any dependency is unhealthy
    res.status(503).json({
      status: 'unhealthy',
      error: err.message,
    });
  }
});
```

#### Metrics to Track

| Metric | Why it matters |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Request rate** | Spot traffic spikes |
| **Error rate** (5xx/total) | Detect breaking changes fast |
| **P95/P99 latency** | Identify slow queries before users complain |
| **CPU/Memory usage** | Know when to scale |
| **DB connection pool size** | Detect connection leaks |

#### Alerting Principles

- Alert on **symptoms** (high error rate, high latency), not **causes** (high CPU). Users don't care about CPU.
- Every alert should be actionable — if you can't do anything about it, it shouldn't page you.
- Set **on-call runbooks**: a document describing what to do when alert X fires.

